1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:02,110
Hi everyone, I'm Ruby Agarwal.

2
00:00:03,024 --> 00:00:08,866
It's great to be here today, and I hope
you're all having a good day wherever

3
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:10,335
in the world you're joining from.

4
00:00:10,351 --> 00:00:17,015
I'm going to guess that many of you have
spent years doing what I've been doing,

5
00:00:17,207 --> 00:00:24,192
which is trying to make our platforms
smarter, faster, and more automated.

6
00:00:24,988 --> 00:00:32,033
And just when we thought we were getting
pretty good at self-service, along come AI

7
00:00:32,085 --> 00:00:39,060
agents, changing every single assumption
we built around those platforms.

8
00:00:40,189 --> 00:00:43,164
And that's what I want
to talk about today.

9
00:00:43,249 --> 00:00:48,492
So for years, we've focused on
making it easier for engineers

10
00:00:48,497 --> 00:00:55,003
to deploy applications, provision
infrastructure, recover from failures

11
00:00:55,332 --> 00:00:58,274
without waiting in ticket queues.

12
00:00:59,939 --> 00:01:05,464
But underneath that automation was an
assumption that someone had already

13
00:01:05,471 --> 00:01:08,070
decided what needed to happen.

14
00:01:09,707 --> 00:01:16,984
But now, these agents can make
those decisions themselves, and that

15
00:01:17,031 --> 00:01:20,194
changes the platform's responsibility.

16
00:01:20,334 --> 00:01:25,892
It's no longer just about whether
an action can execute safely, it's

17
00:01:26,265 --> 00:01:31,634
also about whether the agent should
have the authority to decide, the

18
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:37,488
authority to take that action, and
make sure that that a-action happens.

19
00:01:37,543 --> 00:01:40,003
Let me take you to a room with an example.

20
00:01:40,521 --> 00:01:42,262
It's your incident bridge.

21
00:01:42,985 --> 00:01:45,200
It's two fourteen in the morning.

22
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:50,022
A deployment just went out,
let's say, eight minutes ago.

23
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:55,901
The P ninety-nine latency has jumped
through the roof two hundred and twenty.

24
00:01:55,923 --> 00:02:00,188
Your error rates jumped from one
point eight percent to seven point

25
00:02:00,226 --> 00:02:04,445
four percent, and your customers
are starting to see the impact.

26
00:02:04,772 --> 00:02:06,842
You're starting to get your support calls.

27
00:02:07,589 --> 00:02:11,720
If you've been on such a
call, you know this situation.

28
00:02:12,912 --> 00:02:14,933
What is our priority at that point?

29
00:02:15,059 --> 00:02:21,234
Our priority is restoring the
service, and that too very quickly.

30
00:02:22,234 --> 00:02:27,717
Now let's imagine an AI agent that
is monitoring this production system.

31
00:02:28,572 --> 00:02:33,378
It correlated the deployment with
the metrics, the traces, the rep-

32
00:02:34,027 --> 00:02:38,941
the logs, the state of the system,
the fact that it was upgraded eight

33
00:02:38,942 --> 00:02:45,458
minutes ago, and comes back with 96%
confidence that the new release is

34
00:02:45,464 --> 00:02:51,309
what caused the degradation, and its
recommendation is immediate rollback.

35
00:02:52,754 --> 00:02:55,907
On the surface, that
does sound reasonable.

36
00:02:55,974 --> 00:03:03,441
We want immediate service restoration,
but then it may not have all

37
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:05,159
the context about the system.

38
00:03:05,950 --> 00:03:08,051
Let's put in some additional context.

39
00:03:08,742 --> 00:03:11,981
The release included a big
database schema change.

40
00:03:12,229 --> 00:03:17,849
The previous version had a more
aggressive retry policy, and the

41
00:03:17,849 --> 00:03:24,197
services are sharing a database with
other customer-facing applications.

42
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:27,405
So we've just added a few
more layers of context.

43
00:03:27,904 --> 00:03:31,656
Suddenly, this rollback
is not so straightforward.

44
00:03:32,794 --> 00:03:36,943
The agent may be absolutely right
about the cause of the degradation,

45
00:03:37,265 --> 00:03:44,237
but that doesn't make rollback the
immediate automatic intervention.

46
00:03:44,259 --> 00:03:49,344
Just because the diagnosis is
correct, it doesn't automatically

47
00:03:49,404 --> 00:03:52,218
establish the authority to act.

48
00:03:52,241 --> 00:03:56,720
Think about how we've built
self-service platforms.

49
00:03:58,101 --> 00:04:03,417
We've invested in APIs, we've
invested in our CI/CD pipelines,

50
00:04:03,777 --> 00:04:07,859
IaC, or infrastructure as code,
we're all very proud of that.

51
00:04:08,357 --> 00:04:13,009
We've got developer portals, Kubernetes
operators, and policy engines.

52
00:04:14,051 --> 00:04:17,125
But in all of this, how have we built it?

53
00:04:17,945 --> 00:04:24,732
An engineer decides what they need, the
platform checks identity, permissions,

54
00:04:24,804 --> 00:04:27,525
policies, and execution constraints.

55
00:04:27,811 --> 00:04:32,947
And yes, autonomous decisions
are not entirely new.

56
00:04:33,034 --> 00:04:37,447
We've had autoscalers, we've got
Kubernetes controllers, and they've

57
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:40,900
made operational decisions for years.

58
00:04:42,865 --> 00:04:49,234
But their behavior is usually
defined through relatively specific

59
00:04:49,234 --> 00:04:54,620
and explicit rules or desired
state machine or state models.

60
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:01,241
With the AI agents, the difference
is that they can interpret multiple

61
00:05:01,252 --> 00:05:06,204
signals, they can form a hypothesis,
select an intervention, and generate

62
00:05:06,205 --> 00:05:09,426
a request we may not have anticipated.

63
00:05:10,409 --> 00:05:17,519
The way I think about it, I would separate
the platform contract into three parts.

64
00:05:19,327 --> 00:05:19,950
Decision.

65
00:05:21,086 --> 00:05:25,108
What does the agent believe
should happen and why?

66
00:05:26,108 --> 00:05:26,878
Authority.

67
00:05:28,014 --> 00:05:34,517
Has the agent been delegated the right to
make that decision under these conditions?

68
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:40,135
Once again, has the agent been delegated
the right to make that decision

69
00:05:40,157 --> 00:05:42,486
under these specific conditions?

70
00:05:44,188 --> 00:05:46,395
And the third, execution.

71
00:05:47,344 --> 00:05:52,087
Can the platform safely perform
the authorized action against

72
00:05:52,128 --> 00:05:54,412
the current production state?

73
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:59,876
I don't want the same agent
diagnosing a problem, granting

74
00:05:59,876 --> 00:06:04,770
itself authority, executing the
change, and declaring success.

75
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:10,690
All of these responsibilities
need independent boundaries.

76
00:06:11,906 --> 00:06:17,455
The agent proposes, the authority
mechanism constraints, and the

77
00:06:17,473 --> 00:06:20,608
platform executes and verifies.

78
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:26,777
That's the shift from validating
execution to validating a

79
00:06:26,808 --> 00:06:30,789
machine-generated intent by an agent.

80
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:37,070
Let's make this practical I wouldn't give
a remediation agent permanent permission

81
00:06:37,073 --> 00:06:41,659
to roll back any production service
whenever it thinks something is wrong.

82
00:06:42,321 --> 00:06:47,610
Instead, I would delegate authority
for very specific decisions

83
00:06:47,997 --> 00:06:50,000
within the explicit limits.

84
00:06:51,306 --> 00:06:54,906
Think of it as a short-lived lease.

85
00:06:55,194 --> 00:07:00,566
For example, an agent may be authorized
to roll back one stateless service to a

86
00:07:00,566 --> 00:07:08,321
verified previous version, but only if
the evidence is fresh, the rollback is

87
00:07:08,356 --> 00:07:13,418
compatible with the current state, and
no conflicting remediation is active

88
00:07:14,418 --> 00:07:20,643
That authorization, the time for that
should also have an expiry because

89
00:07:20,854 --> 00:07:23,638
our production doesn't stand still.

90
00:07:24,025 --> 00:07:28,395
Production moves its state over time.

91
00:07:29,371 --> 00:07:32,254
At two fifteen, the rollback
might be reasonable.

92
00:07:32,903 --> 00:07:36,678
At two sixteen, the incident
commander may have shifted your

93
00:07:36,678 --> 00:07:41,658
traffic, or maybe another controller
is already recovering the service.

94
00:07:42,531 --> 00:07:50,718
So an approval based on an earlier state
may no longer even apply, and therefore,

95
00:07:51,148 --> 00:07:59,557
authority needs scope, conditions,
expiration, and revocation, along with

96
00:07:59,655 --> 00:08:02,900
checks immediately before execution.

97
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,560
We're delegating a
decision within boundaries.

98
00:08:07,828 --> 00:08:12,254
We're not handing an agent
permanent control of production.

99
00:08:13,475 --> 00:08:18,056
Once again, we're delegating
a decision within boundaries.

100
00:08:18,271 --> 00:08:22,107
We're not handing an agent
permanent control of production.

101
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:26,234
Let's look at this, how it
would work architecturally.

102
00:08:27,873 --> 00:08:38,187
I think of it in four steps: propose,
adjudicate, commit, and verify First,

103
00:08:38,526 --> 00:08:41,497
the agent proposes a structured intent.

104
00:08:42,486 --> 00:08:46,319
Not just saying, "Hey, production's
unhealthy, let's roll back."

105
00:08:46,748 --> 00:08:53,137
No, it has to identify the service, the
current and target versions, have the

106
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:58,913
supporting evidence, tell me what the
expected customer impact is, and the

107
00:08:58,915 --> 00:09:06,079
conditions that should actually stop
the operation Second, an independent

108
00:09:06,118 --> 00:09:13,773
authority service is going to evaluate the
request that this original agent proposed.

109
00:09:14,151 --> 00:09:18,974
It should check, is this agent
even authorized to give me

110
00:09:18,975 --> 00:09:20,571
this kind of recommendation?

111
00:09:20,670 --> 00:09:23,965
So the agent's identity,
the delegated scope.

112
00:09:24,368 --> 00:09:25,893
Is the evidence fresh?

113
00:09:25,903 --> 00:09:31,254
How, how long ago was this decision
or evidence provided to me?

114
00:09:31,829 --> 00:09:34,600
check the dependencies
of the whole system.

115
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:36,726
Is the dependency healthy?

116
00:09:36,989 --> 00:09:39,521
what's the blast radius of this change?

117
00:09:39,912 --> 00:09:44,808
Is there something else going on, some
other remediation happening in parallel?

118
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:53,575
Once it's gone through these conditions,
it can say allow, deny, or escalate Let's

119
00:09:53,575 --> 00:09:58,213
say it came back with allow, and we move
on to the next phase, which is commit.

120
00:09:58,522 --> 00:09:59,339
Execution.

121
00:10:00,616 --> 00:10:05,976
This executor then again has to
validate the authorization and

122
00:10:05,976 --> 00:10:11,130
recheck the critical conditions
before actually committing the action.

123
00:10:12,071 --> 00:10:15,752
This matters because production
may have changed once again between

124
00:10:15,790 --> 00:10:18,072
authorization and execution.

125
00:10:18,103 --> 00:10:23,775
It's the classic time of check
versus time of use problem.

126
00:10:24,066 --> 00:10:29,614
Resource versions, locks, fencing
mechanisms, all of these can

127
00:10:29,615 --> 00:10:31,703
help prevent conflicting changes.

128
00:10:32,709 --> 00:10:34,998
And finally, verify.

129
00:10:36,419 --> 00:10:40,488
And this is something I strongly
believe in when it comes

130
00:10:40,488 --> 00:10:41,962
to production engineering.

131
00:10:42,962 --> 00:10:48,853
A successful API call is not
proof of successful recovery.

132
00:10:49,601 --> 00:10:54,087
The rollback API may have reported
success, but the customer journey could

133
00:10:54,092 --> 00:10:58,565
still be degraded or another shared
dependency might now be under pressure.

134
00:10:59,071 --> 00:11:03,698
So we really have to verify the
operational outcome, not just the

135
00:11:03,708 --> 00:11:11,918
successful execution of a particular
API or a command On top of that, the

136
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:17,255
platform needs to preserve the evidence
that connects the proposed intent on

137
00:11:17,257 --> 00:11:23,227
the first step to the authorization, the
actual action that was taken, and then

138
00:11:23,230 --> 00:11:26,276
the final result, the observed result.

139
00:11:27,095 --> 00:11:29,606
All of these have to have an audit log.

140
00:11:29,751 --> 00:11:33,553
All of these have to have
evidence for when you get

141
00:11:33,571 --> 00:11:35,721
into your post-incident phase.

142
00:11:35,771 --> 00:11:39,401
Let's actually focus on that
ninety-six percent confidence.

143
00:11:39,417 --> 00:11:43,260
The deployment happened just
before the incident, and the

144
00:11:43,271 --> 00:11:44,966
agent correlated the two.

145
00:11:45,218 --> 00:11:45,817
Very good.

146
00:11:46,005 --> 00:11:47,855
That is very useful evidence.

147
00:11:48,009 --> 00:11:52,312
But correlation doesn't
necessarily establish causality.

148
00:11:53,395 --> 00:11:57,273
What if the database had
saturated at around the same time?

149
00:11:57,368 --> 00:12:01,420
What if there was a regional
network path that got degraded or a

150
00:12:01,484 --> 00:12:06,738
traffic shift happened that changes
how the workloads are reacting?

151
00:12:08,396 --> 00:12:11,846
All of these scenarios could
produce similar symptoms.

152
00:12:12,334 --> 00:12:18,641
So for consequential changes, we
want evidence that distinguishes

153
00:12:19,273 --> 00:12:26,185
the agent's preferred explanation
and also get alternatives and be

154
00:12:26,185 --> 00:12:28,808
able to evalu-ev-evaluate them.

155
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:34,349
For example, the situation that
happened or the issue that happened,

156
00:12:34,361 --> 00:12:38,284
was it only on the instances that
were running the new version?

157
00:12:39,016 --> 00:12:44,118
Do we have any traces of the
identity or traffic cohort?

158
00:12:44,559 --> 00:12:50,253
Do we have any other information
about the traffic change?

159
00:12:50,506 --> 00:12:57,811
We also know that delay during an
incident is very expensive, both in

160
00:12:57,812 --> 00:13:05,664
terms of trust and customer impact We
can't treat every situation the same.

161
00:13:05,827 --> 00:13:11,116
For example, restarting a replaceable
replica is very different.

162
00:13:11,190 --> 00:13:13,857
It does not need to be
a research pap- paper.

163
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:18,743
But if you're rolling back, let's
say, a shared database service or

164
00:13:18,743 --> 00:13:23,080
something like that, that may impact
multiple services, multiple customers,

165
00:13:23,381 --> 00:13:25,554
that's a very different decision.

166
00:13:26,567 --> 00:13:30,211
So our evidence standard, whatever
evidence we're getting from the

167
00:13:30,211 --> 00:13:36,570
agent, that evidence standard should
also reflect the consequences of

168
00:13:36,599 --> 00:13:38,676
getting the re- decision wrong.

169
00:13:38,749 --> 00:13:39,944
Let's take a step back.

170
00:13:40,699 --> 00:13:44,126
Let's assume the agent was
right and the rollback worked.

171
00:13:45,626 --> 00:13:50,745
Service A returns to the previous
version, our latency improves, our

172
00:13:50,746 --> 00:13:55,725
error rates come down, our dashboards
are starting to show green, and from

173
00:13:55,725 --> 00:13:58,988
the agent's perspective, it succeeded.

174
00:14:00,319 --> 00:14:03,311
But remember the
additional context we had.

175
00:14:03,348 --> 00:14:07,733
The previous version retries more
aggressively, and suddenly we're

176
00:14:07,749 --> 00:14:13,128
hitting the shared database more
aggressively, and within mi- minutes,

177
00:14:13,569 --> 00:14:19,523
these other services, let's call them B
and C, let's say they start degrading.

178
00:14:19,965 --> 00:14:21,018
So what have we done?

179
00:14:21,066 --> 00:14:25,930
We've changed the address of where
the problem was to another address.

180
00:14:26,413 --> 00:14:31,502
We've restored one service, but we've made
the overall customer experience worse.

181
00:14:32,198 --> 00:14:42,887
And this is Very common in a distributed
system that we have today, industry-wide.

182
00:14:44,012 --> 00:14:50,081
Local success doesn't necessarily
mean system-level success.

183
00:14:51,663 --> 00:14:53,715
Let's add another dimension to this.

184
00:14:54,155 --> 00:14:56,606
What if there were two
agents running on the system?

185
00:14:56,914 --> 00:15:01,578
There's a release agent that decides to
roll back while a capacity agent sees

186
00:15:01,591 --> 00:15:03,831
resource pressure and starts scaling.

187
00:15:04,672 --> 00:15:05,433
Well, guess what?

188
00:15:05,456 --> 00:15:08,558
Scaling changed your traffic distribution.

189
00:15:09,229 --> 00:15:12,397
The release agent interpreted
those changes and att- and

190
00:15:12,397 --> 00:15:14,569
attempted another intervention.

191
00:15:15,030 --> 00:15:19,892
Capacity agent saw utilization
fall and scales down again.

192
00:15:20,900 --> 00:15:25,488
And both agents, when they are
making these individual decisions,

193
00:15:25,546 --> 00:15:30,441
they're reasonable decisions, but
together they're creating instability.

194
00:15:31,530 --> 00:15:35,792
And that's why coordination
between autonomous agents,

195
00:15:35,862 --> 00:15:38,028
it cannot be an afterthought.

196
00:15:39,593 --> 00:15:45,145
Just like we build in traditional
recovery systems, we need concurrency

197
00:15:45,150 --> 00:15:49,829
controls, resource ownership, fencing,
cool down periods, and arbitration

198
00:15:49,839 --> 00:15:51,661
when proposed actions conflict.

199
00:15:53,185 --> 00:15:57,439
And during a major incident, we
need the ability for an incident

200
00:15:57,455 --> 00:15:58,868
commander to take control.

201
00:16:00,475 --> 00:16:05,961
The platform has to protect the
overall system, even when individual

202
00:16:06,090 --> 00:16:12,512
agents are optimizing their own
objectives, and that's a fundamentally

203
00:16:12,517 --> 00:16:21,759
different responsibility than simply
making an API available So how do

204
00:16:21,764 --> 00:16:24,174
we introduce autonomy responsibly?

205
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:30,004
I wouldn't start with broad
production execution rights.

206
00:16:30,267 --> 00:16:35,242
I'd expand authority based
on demonstrated outcomes.

207
00:16:35,712 --> 00:16:36,395
Observe.

208
00:16:37,557 --> 00:16:42,268
The agent monitors your production,
builds evidence, but makes no changes.

209
00:16:43,357 --> 00:16:46,054
Then go to your next step, recommend.

210
00:16:46,477 --> 00:16:50,369
The agent can propose a
diagnosis and intervention while

211
00:16:50,369 --> 00:16:52,589
a human approves or declines.

212
00:16:53,984 --> 00:17:00,046
This stage is valuable because we're
measuring performance of your agent

213
00:17:00,053 --> 00:17:06,833
before delegating execution authority
Then comes act within bounds.

214
00:17:07,613 --> 00:17:13,784
Now this agent can perform specific,
very specific use cases, specific

215
00:17:13,785 --> 00:17:20,267
interventions automatically within your
defined services, defined environments,

216
00:17:20,514 --> 00:17:25,943
and risk limits Finally, act and report.

217
00:17:27,042 --> 00:17:32,658
For mature, well-evaluated decision
classes, the agent can operate

218
00:17:32,677 --> 00:17:37,888
within broad-- within broader
delegated boundaries with immediate

219
00:17:37,888 --> 00:17:41,319
reporting and continued oversight.

220
00:17:43,293 --> 00:17:44,444
Here's what matters.

221
00:17:44,979 --> 00:17:49,198
An agent that's excellent at
detecting memory pressure has not

222
00:17:49,416 --> 00:17:54,792
automatically earned authority
to execute database failovers.

223
00:17:56,779 --> 00:18:02,102
What I want you to take away from
here, authority expands by decision

224
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:08,716
class supported by evidence, not
because we broadly trust an agent.

225
00:18:10,041 --> 00:18:15,896
And when performance deteriorates,
we narrow that authority again When

226
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:21,505
reviewing a design for an autonomous
production agent, here are the six

227
00:18:21,542 --> 00:18:23,615
things that you would want to look for.

228
00:18:24,963 --> 00:18:25,748
Evidence.

229
00:18:27,267 --> 00:18:29,786
What supports the proposed action?

230
00:18:30,116 --> 00:18:31,488
What is the context?

231
00:18:31,488 --> 00:18:36,600
What are the ca- what are the
characteristics or, what is the

232
00:18:36,612 --> 00:18:43,037
data that made us-- that made
the agent m- make this decision?

233
00:18:44,132 --> 00:18:45,045
Confidence.

234
00:18:45,625 --> 00:18:49,430
How reliable has this class
of decision actually been?

235
00:18:51,061 --> 00:18:57,701
Blast radius Which services, customers,
or dependencies could be affected?

236
00:18:57,716 --> 00:18:58,807
Very important.

237
00:18:59,578 --> 00:19:01,281
Am I making a narrow scope?

238
00:19:01,536 --> 00:19:06,253
Is this change going to impact a very
narrow scope, or is it the entire system?

239
00:19:08,125 --> 00:19:09,147
Reversibility.

240
00:19:09,348 --> 00:19:12,682
Can we safely undo the action
if the decision was wrong?

241
00:19:13,407 --> 00:19:15,704
Always have a rollback plan in place.

242
00:19:16,890 --> 00:19:17,633
Ownership.

243
00:19:18,297 --> 00:19:22,883
Who delegated this authority, and
who is accountable for the outcome?

244
00:19:24,835 --> 00:19:26,617
And lastly, time window.

245
00:19:27,517 --> 00:19:30,334
How long is the evidence
and authorization valid?

246
00:19:31,079 --> 00:19:34,899
These aren't simply six
scores to average together.

247
00:19:35,245 --> 00:19:37,035
Some of them are hard boundaries.

248
00:19:37,051 --> 00:19:40,397
You have to look at each
one of them individually.

249
00:19:41,060 --> 00:19:45,464
If an agent is authorized to
act on a one service, a high

250
00:19:45,466 --> 00:19:49,630
confidence score doesn't give it
permission to change five services.

251
00:19:50,150 --> 00:19:59,677
And every consequential autonomous action
needs a decision record and audit log.

252
00:20:00,438 --> 00:20:03,614
Traditional audit logs
tell us what executed.

253
00:20:03,733 --> 00:20:10,066
The decision record should also capture
why the action was proposed, what

254
00:20:10,091 --> 00:20:16,378
evidence supported it, which authority
permitted it, and what actually happened.

255
00:20:17,089 --> 00:20:21,006
That's essential for all
your incident reviews, your

256
00:20:21,086 --> 00:20:26,984
auditability, and continuously
improving your autonomy model.

257
00:20:27,092 --> 00:20:31,700
Let's bring this together using
the same rollback recommendation.

258
00:20:32,806 --> 00:20:37,596
Our agent is ninety-six percent
confident that the deployment

259
00:20:37,793 --> 00:20:39,425
caused the degradation.

260
00:20:40,742 --> 00:20:44,272
Now imagine a very different
production condition.

261
00:20:44,993 --> 00:20:49,079
In the first condition, the evidence
is fresh, the shared dependency

262
00:20:49,079 --> 00:20:53,100
is healthy, the action affects one
service, rollback is compatible,

263
00:20:53,336 --> 00:20:55,380
no other remediation is active.

264
00:20:56,039 --> 00:21:00,870
And so under our defined policy, the
platform allows a bounded rollback.

265
00:21:00,908 --> 00:21:04,317
Now consider the same
recommendation, but the health of

266
00:21:04,317 --> 00:21:06,536
the shared database is unknown.

267
00:21:07,492 --> 00:21:11,366
The confidence hasn't changed, but
the platform can't establish that

268
00:21:11,367 --> 00:21:17,239
the action will remain within the
delegated safely boundaries, so

269
00:21:17,240 --> 00:21:19,200
the decision changes to escalate.

270
00:21:19,251 --> 00:21:23,058
Next, imagine the e-evidence
is eighteen minutes old.

271
00:21:24,914 --> 00:21:29,330
During a production incident,
eighteen minutes is a long time.

272
00:21:29,337 --> 00:21:32,803
Traffic could have shifted,
dependencies could have changed.

273
00:21:33,439 --> 00:21:37,619
Under this policy, the request
is denied until the agent

274
00:21:37,953 --> 00:21:41,413
reassesses with fresh evidence.

275
00:21:41,431 --> 00:21:48,635
Principle is that authority is evaluated
in context rather than permanently

276
00:21:48,672 --> 00:21:51,426
attached to an agent's identity.

277
00:21:51,531 --> 00:21:57,840
Let's talk about ownership, because
even the best technical architecture

278
00:21:57,844 --> 00:22:02,910
won't work if nobody is accountable
for how autonomy is governed.

279
00:22:04,270 --> 00:22:06,573
So where do I see responsibilities?

280
00:22:06,958 --> 00:22:12,684
Platform engineering, they own the
mechanisms, identity, policy enforcement,

281
00:22:12,694 --> 00:22:19,114
scoped authorization, execution
controls, revocation, and traceability.

282
00:22:20,819 --> 00:22:26,880
SRE and service owners, they define
operational safety, sufficient

283
00:22:26,884 --> 00:22:32,406
evidence, safe recovery actions,
dependency constraints, and

284
00:22:32,414 --> 00:22:34,847
verification of customer outcomes.

285
00:22:36,380 --> 00:22:41,232
And engineering leadership and risk
owners, they govern delegation, which

286
00:22:41,239 --> 00:22:47,476
decisions can be autonomous, which require
approval, and who owns the outcome.

287
00:22:47,507 --> 00:22:49,895
You know, we all talk
about human in the loop.

288
00:22:50,136 --> 00:22:52,220
There's a trap we should avoid.

289
00:22:53,355 --> 00:22:56,576
We add a human approval
button to every agent.

290
00:22:57,032 --> 00:23:01,022
People will receive dozens of
recommendations, and eventually

291
00:23:01,043 --> 00:23:03,079
approving becomes automatic.

292
00:23:03,318 --> 00:23:06,974
That human in the loop at that
point is just a r-rubber stamp.

293
00:23:07,088 --> 00:23:09,040
It's not meaningful oversight.

294
00:23:09,086 --> 00:23:13,637
All we've done is added another click
between the agent and the production.

295
00:23:14,868 --> 00:23:20,804
I would rather that as we are defining our
AI governance, establish clear boundaries,

296
00:23:21,148 --> 00:23:26,326
independently enforce them, measure
outcomes, and escalate the decisions.

297
00:23:27,326 --> 00:23:31,151
Human accountability doesn't
mean approving every action.

298
00:23:31,196 --> 00:23:35,087
It means owning the authority
that we have delegated.

299
00:23:36,087 --> 00:23:37,347
Let me close with this.

300
00:23:38,628 --> 00:23:44,875
Platform engineering has spent years
removing friction from execution.

301
00:23:46,511 --> 00:23:54,652
We've made it easier to deploy, provision,
scale, and recover, and AI gives us

302
00:23:54,654 --> 00:24:00,964
an opportunity to automate more of
those decisions behind those actions.

303
00:24:01,787 --> 00:24:04,497
I think that's a tremendous opportunity.

304
00:24:06,257 --> 00:24:12,115
But as we delegate more decisions
to autonomous systems, we have to be

305
00:24:12,484 --> 00:24:19,077
equally deliberate about authority
because sometimes a production failure

306
00:24:19,141 --> 00:24:24,325
doesn't happen because the system
executed something incorrectly.

307
00:24:25,325 --> 00:24:29,179
It executed exactly what
it was permitted to do.

308
00:24:31,017 --> 00:24:35,625
The point is the decision to do
it was the part we got wrong.

309
00:24:37,432 --> 00:24:42,776
So when an autonomous agent proposes a
production change, the platform should

310
00:24:42,790 --> 00:24:52,381
be able to explain why it is saying yes
Self-service asked, "Can I do this?"

311
00:24:53,815 --> 00:25:01,775
Safe autonomy adds, "Should I be allowed
to decide that this needs to be done?"

312
00:25:02,337 --> 00:25:08,752
And that is the authority shift, and
I believe it's an important part of

313
00:25:08,769 --> 00:25:11,756
where platform engineering goes next.

314
00:25:12,762 --> 00:25:13,207
Thank you

